.planning/research/.cache/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/
# `.env*`, not `.env`. The exact name ignored the one file anybody thinks to
# create and left every neighbour committable -- `.env.bak`, `.env.local`,
# `.env.backup`, and the `.env.env.bak` an editing script actually produced on
# 2026-08-14, which held three live credentials and showed up in `git status`
# as an untracked file one `git add -A` away from the history. The example file
# is re-admitted below, because it is a shipped format sample and must stay
# visible for the same reason `context/*.example.json` does.
.env*
!.env.example
*.wav
*.mp3
*.m4a
sessions/

# STT benchmark harness (Phase 1) — real audio, human transcripts and per-error
# detail must never reach git. See benchmark/README.md.
benchmark/data/

# Phase 4 (04-01-PLAN.md §C8) — context/brief.json names a real customer,
# labelled evidence vs. inference; context/survey.json is what the user plans
# to ask them. The most sensitive plaintext this repository has ever
# produced. Only the *.example.json format samples (synthetic content) ship.
#
# Phase 5 (05-01-PLAN.md §K1) — context/facts.json (claims extracted from a
# real interview, carrying verbatim quoted speech) and context/hypotheses.json
# (user-authored) are covered by the SAME rule below; no new rule is needed.
# Before moving either file out of context/, read this: that edit is exactly
# how customer speech would leak into git.
context/*
!context/*.example.json

# Phase A -- the project tree. Verified with real `git check-ignore` BEFORE
# any code wrote here: `projects/<n>/sessions/` was already covered, because
# the `sessions/` rule above is unanchored and matches at any depth. But
# `projects/<n>/context/` was NOT -- `context/*` is root-anchored and does
# not follow. Briefs, surveys, hypotheses and extracted facts under
# `projects/` were committable.
#
# BLANKET, and it must stay blanket. The tempting shape is `projects/*` plus
# `!projects/*/context/*.example.json` to keep shipping format samples from
# inside the tree; it CANNOT work. Git does not descend into an excluded
# directory, so a negation below it is never consulted. The format samples
# stay where they are, in the root `context/`, which is why this rule can
# afford to be total. Gate 29 asserts it over concrete artifact paths.
projects/

# The coach eval's GENERATED artifacts (benchmark/eval/). The interview script
# is source and ships; everything below is rebuilt from it by `build_audio.py`
# in about two minutes, and the audio alone is ~145 MB. `*.wav` above already
# covers the audio at any depth -- these are the rest.
#
# `claudictive_truth.json` is derived, not authored: it is the script plus the
# timings SAPI happened to produce on one machine, so committing it would
# invite a later reader to trust it as a reference when the voices, the rate or
# the gap constants have since changed.
benchmark/eval/turns/
benchmark/eval/turns.json
benchmark/eval/claudictive_truth.json
benchmark/eval/*.log

# Full-screen captures from the `snap` skill. These are photographs of the
# operator's whole desktop -- other windows, file names, whatever else was
# open -- taken to check how the overlay renders. Never committable.
Screenshots/

# Model-replay outputs (benchmark/eval/model_replay.py). These carry
# `trigger_text` -- the interviewee's own words, lifted verbatim out of a
# recorded transcript -- alongside the questions each model produced. The
# transcript they come from is already ignored under the project tree, and a
# replay of it must not be the hole that walks the same speech back into the
# repository under a different name.
benchmark/eval/replay_*.jsonl
benchmark/eval/replay_*.summary.json
benchmark/eval/replay_*.log
